Title: Document the clipboard as a third diagnostic-channel sink in SPEC.md
Created: 2026-09-06
Author: sim

--- log ---
2026-09-06T15:36Z sim created

--- body ---

## Context

SPEC.md's R22 block (added by ticket 0698, PR #386) enumerates the sitter's
diagnostic channel's sinks -- the volatile ring and Zotero's debug output --
and states their shared privacy rule (no text, titles or source paths;
`error.name` only). Ticket 0693 (PR #388) added a third sink: a "copy
journal" clipboard action, already part of 0693's own authorized Design
section (not scope creep -- the ticket explicitly planned a copy action). It
correctly applies the same privacy rule (scrubs `rootURI` from ring records
before serializing to the clipboard, proven by a positive-control test that
first shows the unscrubbed defect, then the fix).

What's missing is SPEC.md catching up: the R22 paragraph still describes only
two sinks. A reader relying on SPEC.md as the authoritative description of
where diagnostic data can go would miss the clipboard entirely.

## Work

- Add one sentence to SPEC.md's R22 diagnostic-channel paragraph (near the
  existing sink enumeration, likely after line ~2261) naming the clipboard
  copy action as a third sink, and stating it inherits the same privacy rule
  (proof: `bench/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js`'s `composeSDTJournalReport`, which
  scrubs `rootURI` from each ring record before serialization).
- Bump SPEC.md's `Date:` header per this document's date-is-version
  convention.
- Cross-check `verification/SDT-SITTER-LAUNCH.md` for the same gap (it may
  also need the clipboard action named, per its existing "how to read the
  sitter's state" content from ticket 0698).

## Acceptance

- [ ] SPEC.md names the clipboard as a third sink, with the same privacy
      rule stated or cross-referenced (not restated in different words --
      see AGENTS.md's "one statement per fact").
- [ ] `bench/check_progress.py` run before and after to confirm no
      unintended requirement-count change (this is design prose extending
      an existing paragraph, not a new numbered requirement).

## Scope

Pure documentation, no code change -- the code already implements the
correct behavior (verified in PR #388's own review). Small, matches the
pattern of ticket 0698.

